<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Junípero Serra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1713">1713</a>–<a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Junípero Serra was a Spanish Franciscan friar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> who founded the first nine of the 21 Spanish missions of Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> in 1769. He had earlier worked as a missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s Sierra Gorda. Canonised in 2015, he is also criticised for the coercion and disease that the mission system brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigenous-peoples-of-california">Native Californians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/junipero-serra.jpg" alt="Junípero Serra" width="320" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engraving_of_Jun%C3%ADpero_Serra_(1787)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
